{
 "_note": "The pose library of the placeholder fighter (ROADMAP M3.3c; ADR-019 D2, D6). make_move_clips.py reads this file and fighter_a.json and keys one stepped pose per frame for every attack and every reserved cycle; capture_pose.py writes a pose refined at the viewport back here. Bone keys are the semantic names of rig_bones.json, never a Rigify name.",
 "_axes": "A bone's value is [elevation, azimuth, twist] in degrees: WHERE THE BONE POINTS in the world. Elevation is above the horizontal plane (90 straight up, -90 straight down); azimuth is from the opponent (+X, 0) toward the bone's own side (an l_ or center bone: the fighter's left; an r_ bone: its right), so a mirrored pose is the same numbers and 180 is away from the opponent; twist is about the bone's own axis, relative to the parent's, counter-clockwise looking down the bone, mirrored for r_ bones. A bone left out follows its parent. A captured pose is {\"q\": [w, x, y, z]}, the rotation in the parent's posed frame that capture_pose.py reads back. `hips_drop` lowers the root by that many pixels; the root never moves in the ground plane (D2). `base` starts from another pose; `mirror_of` swaps left and right. At rest: torso and head point up, thighs and shins down, feet forward and a little down, shoulders and arms sideways (Rigify's A-pose). A cycle with a `walk` entry gets its legs from the generator's gait at the kernel's walk speed (make_move_clips.walk_gait); its keys pose the rest of the body.",
